}
```

review_comments:
If this is in `CollectionsMarshal` vs say having a setter on `.Count` does the array want to be cleared?

e.g. it could enable
```csharp
var preCount = list.Count;
// Only include first item for api that takes `List<T>`
CollectionsMarshal.SetCount(list, 1);
MyListApi((IReadOnlyList)list);
//Restore all previous elements
CollectionsMarshal.SetCount(list, preCount);
```

Unsafe apis are to allow users to potentially do the wrong thing

I'd personally skip the clearing in `CollectionsMarshal` then add setter to `.Count` that has clearing
```csharp
public int Count 
{
    get => _size;
    set 
    {
         // Resize Count but with clearing
    }
}
```
Then the `CollectionsMarshal` parallel has the unsafe component (if you need to temporally reduce size, then increase after but not loose elements)

@eiriktsarpalis, The general issue is that observing that semantic requires something like `InternalsVisibleTo` or relying on implementation details of how `SetCount` works.

There isn't a way to just observe the backing array of the list from the test, so at best we'd be validating that `SetCount(3)`, followed by `SetCount(5)` "preserves" the value for value types and clears the value for reference types.

We do not similarly test that references get cleared for cases like `Remove`, which are much more important and mainline scenarios.

If we expect the most common use case will be increasing the count, should we instead reorder the method to something like:
```C#
public static void SetCount<T>(List<T> list, int count)
{
    if (count >= list._size)
    {
        if (count > list._items.Length)
        {
            list.Grow(count);
        }
    }
    else
    {
        if (count < 0)
        {
            ThrowHelper.ThrowArgumentOutOfRangeException_NeedNonNegNum(nameof(count));
        }

        if (RuntimeHelpers.IsReferenceOrContainsReferences<T>())
        {
            Array.Clear(list._items, count, list._size - count);
        }
    }

    list._size = count;
    list._version++;
}
```
?  This will still increment the version if the count doesn't change, but I don't think that's much different than `list[i] = list[i]`, which will also increment the version.
